From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] integrity: T2 cert-skip quirk is informational, not an error

load_uefi_certs() deliberately skips reading the UEFI db/dbx variables on
Apple T2 Macs because doing so page-faults Apple's firmware and disables EFI
runtime services (see the uefi_skip_cert quirk).  Taking that quirk is the
intended, healthy path, yet it is reported at error level:

    integrity: Reading UEFI Secure Boot Certs is not supported on T2 Macs.

Every affected machine therefore logs a scary KERN_ERR line on every boot
for something that is working exactly as designed.  Move it to pr_info:
the condition is real but it is not a fault, and nothing depends on the
level.  A boot with no Secure Boot certs loaded is normal on these systems.

Not a suppression of a hardware error: this quirk only skips reading EFI
variables that would crash the firmware.  Signing/signature enforcement is
unchanged (and was never active on these machines).

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/security/integrity/platform_certs/load_uefi.c b/security/integrity/platform_certs/load_uefi.c
index c0d6948..1aecba7 100644
--- a/security/integrity/platform_certs/load_uefi.c
+++ b/security/integrity/platform_certs/load_uefi.c
@@ -169,7 +169,7 @@ static int __init load_uefi_certs(void)
 
 	dmi_id = dmi_first_match(uefi_skip_cert);
 	if (dmi_id) {
-		pr_err("Reading UEFI Secure Boot Certs is not supported on T2 Macs.\n");
+		pr_info("Reading UEFI Secure Boot Certs is not supported on T2 Macs.\n");
 		return false;
 	}
 
